 \section{Summary of findings}
 
+The main takeaway is that, in our setting, the prior carried by the feature names influences the model's predictions more than the choice of demonstrations does: flipping three names costs 0.20 \AUROC, while the hand-designed strategies typically differ from balanced random demonstrations by only 0.025.
 The findings are stated in bold throughout, numbered by chapter, and always for the conditions we tested.
 The main ones are these below.
 
 \begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
-  \item \textbf{Flipping feature names hurts more than any shift of the data}: 0.20 \AUROC, against 0.12 for reversing the shortcut and 0.02 (not significant) for covariate shift (\autoref{ch:shift}).
-  \item \textbf{Strategies that neutralise the shortcut help under its reversal more than under covariate shift} (interaction +0.11, Holm $p = 0.024$), but no strategy helps under flipped names (\autoref{ch:shift}).
-  \item \textbf{A trained selector that wins on a stand-in learner does not win on the model}; none of the six pre-registered selector contrasts favours it (\autoref{ch:sata}).
-  \item \textbf{The answer forms in the last six of 28 layers, and the names decide it there}; the model's prior is decodable from layer 2, while the demonstrated rule hardly is (\autoref{ch:mechanism}).
+  \item \textbf{Flipping feature names hurts more than any shift of the data, and far more than the choice of demonstrations helps}: 0.20 \AUROC, against 0.12 for reversing the shortcut and 0.02 (not significant) for covariate shift (\autoref{ch:shift}).
