+\paragraph{Semantic-prior conflict, not concept shift.}
+Conventional concept shift changes $P(y \mid x)$ between the data a learner is shown and the data it is tested on.
+We do not test it: if the queries followed a different rule from the demonstrations, the demonstrations would simply be wrong, and no choice of them could help.
+Instead, the rule stays the same in every environment, and the naming (next section) sets the model's semantic prior against it.
+Flipped names leave the data, the rule, the demonstrations and the queries unchanged and alter only what three features are called, so that the mapping the model brings from pre-training (a higher credit score means approval) points the opposite way to the task's.
+We call this \emph{semantic-prior conflict}.
+It resembles concept shift only from the model's perspective: what changes is the model's prior about $P(y \mid x)$, not $P(y \mid x)$ itself.
 
 \section{Naming: setting the prior against the rule}
 \label{sec:naming}
